    Our first result is a basic LSH scheme for the discrete Fréchet distance, which leads to a very efficient LSH with approximation factor that is linear in the number of curve vertices. The scheme is described in Section 3 and it is surprisingly si mple: We snap the curves to a randomly shifted grid and remove consecutive duplicate ve rtices. It turns out that this simple scheme alleviates the alignment problem which sets t he Fréchet distance computation apart from the l∞-distance. Next, we show in Section 4 that it is even possible to get constant approximation, at the cost of a lower collision pro bability for near curves. The second scheme randomly perturbs the vertices of the input cu rves independently and snaps the vertices to a fixed grid instead of a randomly shifted grid . It is natural to ask if there exists an LSH scheme exhibiting a full-spectrum trade-off be tween collision probability and approximation. We positively answer to this question in Sec tion 5, with a scheme based on a random partition of the input curves, inspired by Indyk’s data structure [17], followed by the application of the basic scheme to each subsequence inde pendently.3 All the LSH schemes achieve zero false-positives, meaning t hat no collisions happen between far curves. When applied to solve the ( c, r)-near neighbor problem, we obtain the results summarized in Table 1 (see also Section 2.3). It is in teresting to compare our bounds with the state of the art. The basic scheme of Theorem 7 provid es a data structure using almost linear space and O(mlogn) query time by allow"

